\begin{definition}[Shoup's generic group model]
Let $p$ be a prime and let $S$ be a label space with $|S| \ge p$. Sample a uniformly random injective map $\mathsf{enc} : \mathbb{Z}_p \to S$. Every algorithm --- honest party and adversary alike --- is given access to one oracle $\mathcal{O}_{\mathsf{enc}}$ supporting two kinds of query:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathsf{enc}(x)$, for $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$, which returns the label $\mathsf{enc}(x) \in S$;
  \item $\mathsf{Add}(c_1, c_2, \sigma_1, \sigma_2)$, for $c_1, c_2 \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ and $\sigma_1, \sigma_2 \in S$, which returns $\mathsf{enc}(c_1 x_1 + c_2 x_2)$ when $\sigma_1 = \mathsf{enc}(x_1)$ and $\sigma_2 = \mathsf{enc}(x_2)$ for some $x_1, x_2 \in \mathbb{Z}_p$, and $\bot$ otherwise.
\end{itemize}
Labels are explicit bit strings: an algorithm may perform arbitrary (unbounded) local computation on the labels it holds. The cost of an algorithm is its number of oracle queries; local computation is free.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Three-party NIKE in an idealized model]
A three-party non-interactive key-exchange protocol relative to an oracle $\mathcal{O}$ is a pair of oracle algorithms $\Pi = (\mathsf{Msg}, \mathsf{Key})$. Each party $i \in [3]$ samples private randomness $\mathsf{r}_i$ and broadcasts a single message $\mathsf{m}_i \leftarrow \mathsf{Msg}^{\mathcal{O}}(1^\lambda, i, \mathsf{r}_i)$; all three messages are sent simultaneously, and $\mathsf{tran} := (\mathsf{m}_1, \mathsf{m}_2, \mathsf{m}_3)$. Without any further interaction, party $i$ outputs $\mathsf{key}_i \leftarrow \mathsf{Key}^{\mathcal{O}}(i, \mathsf{r}_i, \mathsf{tran})$. The protocol is \emph{$n$-query} if, for each $i$, the total number of oracle queries made by $\mathsf{Msg}^{\mathcal{O}}(1^\lambda, i, \cdot)$ and $\mathsf{Key}^{\mathcal{O}}(i, \cdot, \cdot)$ together is at most $n$. It has \emph{completeness error} $\delta$ if
\[
  \Pr\!\left[\mathsf{key}_1 = \mathsf{key}_2 = \mathsf{key}_3\right] \;\ge\; 1 - \delta ,
\]
the probability being over $\mathsf{r}_1, \mathsf{r}_2, \mathsf{r}_3$, the oracle, and the algorithms' coins.
\end{definition}
